@page "/docs"
@attribute [FollowsModeSwitch]
@inject Registry Registry
@inject ApiMetadata Api

@*
    The front door to the docs: what IzzyBlazor is, a first look at the code, one live example
    under the page's mode switch, how it's built, what's in the box (from components.json), what it
    needs, and where else to look. The sidebar covers the docs sections, so this page doesn't
    repeat them as cards.
*@

<PageMeta Title="Docs · IzzyBlazor" Description="The IzzyBlazor docs: a Blazor component library built for static SSR that works in every render mode. Start here to learn how it works." />

<article class="site-article">
    <Stack Gap="3" Class="site-pagehead">
        <Heading Level="1" Size="TextSize.Xl3">Documentation</Heading>
        <TextBlock Size="TextSize.Lg" Color="Color.Muted">
            IzzyBlazor is a Blazor component library built for static SSR. Its components render as HTML, and a small script makes them interactive in the browser. When you choose an interactive render mode, they also bind to your C# and raise events.
        </TextBlock>
    </Stack>

    <Row Gap="2" Align="Align.Center" Class="site-install">
        <TextBlock As="span" Color="Color.Muted" aria-hidden="true">$</TextBlock>
        <Code>@Install</Code>
        <CopyButton Text="@Install" Label="Copy the install command" Size="ControlSize.Sm" />
    </Row>
    <TextBlock>You use the same components on every page. This form posts and validates on <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">static pages</Anchor>, and runs over a circuit on interactive pages, with the same markup. Its model receives the post through <Code>[SupplyParameterFromForm]</Code>:</TextBlock>
    <Code Block Language="razor">@FirstLook</Code>
    <Row Gap="3" Wrap>
        <Button Href="docs/get-started">Get started</Button>
        <Button Href="docs/components" Variant="ButtonVariant.Outline" Accent="Accent.Neutral">Browse components</Button>
    </Row>

    <Heading Level="2">Static · Interactive</Heading>
    <TextBlock>Every example on this site runs in both of Blazor's render modes. To pick one, use the switch, here and on component pages:</TextBlock>
    <List Gap="2">
        <ListItem><strong>Static</strong>: the server renders the page, and IzzyBlazor's script adds keyboard handling, live values and similar behavior. The page needs no circuit and no WebAssembly.</ListItem>
        <ListItem><strong>Interactive</strong>: the page runs on Blazor Server, so C# event handlers and <Code>@@bind</Code> work.</ListItem>
    </List>
    <ExampleViewer Example="home-settings" ModeSwitch />

    <Heading Level="2">How it's built</Heading>
    <Grid Columns="new() { Base = 1, Md = 2 }" Gap="6" Class="site-concepts">
        <Stack Gap="1">
            <Heading Level="3" Size="TextSize.Md" OnThisPage="false" Class="site-concept-title">One set for every render mode</Heading>
            <TextBlock Color="Color.Muted">The same components work on static, Server, WebAssembly and Auto pages. Every page is working HTML before its scripts run. <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">Render modes</Anchor></TextBlock>
        </Stack>
        <Stack Gap="1">
            <Heading Level="3" Size="TextSize.Md" OnThisPage="false" Class="site-concept-title">Forms that post</Heading>
            <TextBlock Color="Color.Muted"><Code>Form</Code> and its fields bind to your model on static pages, through a form post, and on interactive pages. Labels, limits and messages come from your model's attributes. <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms">Forms and validation</Anchor></TextBlock>
        </Stack>
        <Stack Gap="1">
            <Heading Level="3" Size="TextSize.Md" OnThisPage="false" Class="site-concept-title">State in the URL</Heading>
            <TextBlock Color="Color.Muted">Table, FilterForm and Pagination store sorting, filters and the page in the URL. They work without a circuit, and a link to the page keeps them. <Anchor Href="docs/components/table">Table</Anchor></TextBlock>
        </Stack>
        <Stack Gap="1">
            <Heading Level="3" Size="TextSize.Md" OnThisPage="false" Class="site-concept-title">Style props and themes</Heading>
            <TextBlock Color="Color.Muted">Style props set spacing, sizes and colors as parameters. <Code>IzzyTheme</Code> sets the preset, brand and fonts on the server, so the theme is in place on first paint. <Anchor Href="docs/common/style-props">Style props</Anchor> · <Anchor Href="docs/theming/themes">Theming</Anchor></TextBlock>
        </Stack>
        <Stack Gap="1">
            <Heading Level="3" Size="TextSize.Md" OnThisPage="false" Class="site-concept-title">Native elements first</Heading>
            <TextBlock Color="Color.Muted">Buttons, dialogs, selects and disclosures use the browser's own elements. Focus, the keyboard and screen readers work as the platform defines them. Where IzzyBlazor's script adds behavior, it adds the ARIA that goes with it.</TextBlock>
        </Stack>
    </Grid>

    <Heading Level="2">What's in the box</Heading>
    <TextBlock>The library has @Registry.ComponentCountLabel components in @Registry.Categories.Count categories. <Anchor Href="docs/components">See them all</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
    <Grid Columns="new() { Base = 1, Sm = 2, Lg = 3 }" Gap="3" Class="site-box">
        @foreach (var category in Registry.Categories)
        {
            var components = Registry.InCategory(category).ToList();
            <div class="site-box-category">
                <Heading Level="3" Size="TextSize.Md" OnThisPage="false" Class="site-box-title">
                    <Anchor Href="@($"docs/components#{ApiMetadata.Anchor(category).Replace(' ', '-')}")" Underline="AnchorUnderline.Hover">@category</Anchor>
                    <span class="site-box-count">@components.Count<span class="ib-visually-hidden"> components</span></span>
                </Heading>
                <TextBlock Size="TextSize.Sm" Color="Color.Muted">
                    @for (var i = 0; i < Math.Min(4, components.Count); i++)
                    {
                        var component = components[i];
                        @(i == 0 ? "" : ", ")<Anchor Href="@Registry.OverviewHref(component)" Underline="AnchorUnderline.Hover">@component.Name</Anchor>
                    }
                    @if (components.Count > 4)
                    {
                        @($" and {components.Count - 4} more")
                    }
                </TextBlock>
            </div>
        }
    </Grid>

    <Heading Level="2">Requirements</Heading>
    <List Gap="2">
        <ListItem><strong>.NET 10</strong>, in a Blazor Web App. Its pages can be static, Server, WebAssembly or Auto.</ListItem>
        <ListItem><strong>Two packages</strong>: <Code>IzzyBlazor</Code> goes in the server project, and <Code>IzzyBlazor.Core</Code> in a <Code>.Client</Code> project. <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/webassembly-and-auto#packages">Packages</Anchor></ListItem>
        <ListItem><strong>Current browsers</strong>: Chrome and Edge 123+, Firefox 128+, Safari 17.5+. Where a browser supports a newer feature, such as the customizable select, IzzyBlazor uses it.</ListItem>
        <ListItem><strong>MIT licensed</strong>. Pro components are planned.</ListItem>
    </List>

    <Heading Level="2">Resources</Heading>
    <List Gap="2">
        <ListItem><Anchor Href="demos">Demos</Anchor>: realistic scenarios, static and interactive, with the source.</ListItem>
        <ListItem><Anchor Href="https://github.com/enabledevmode/izzyblazor">GitHub</Anchor>: the source and issues.</ListItem>
        <ListItem><Anchor Href="https://github.com/enabledevmode/izzyblazor/releases">Release notes</Anchor>: what changed in each version.</ListItem>
        <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/get-started/ai-assistants">AI assistants</Anchor>: connect yours to the docs' MCP server.</ListItem>
        <ListItem><Anchor Href="izzyblazor.api.json">API metadata</Anchor>: every component's API as JSON, for tools and AI assistants.</ListItem>
    </List>
</article>

@code {
    private const string Install = "dotnet add package IzzyBlazor --prerelease";

    private const string FirstLook = """
        <Form Model="Signup" FormName="signup" OnValidSubmit="SaveAsync">
            <TextField @bind-Value="Signup.Email" />
            <Button Submit>Sign up</Button>
        </Form>

        @code {
            [SupplyParameterFromForm(FormName = "signup")]
            private SignupModel? Signup { get; set; }

            protected override void OnInitialized() => Signup ??= new();
        }
        """;
}
